    "turn_text": "Yeah, and thanks for saying that because I love that we're talking about wife. She's an awesome, awesome person and you say unbothered by it. I would say more than unbothered, proud, happy, grateful. I mean, truly, I could not have gone down the path of public service if not for her incredible hard work, both professionally and of course still the incredible mental load that so many women are saddled with on top of that. I talk about it in a way of trying to normalize it, trying to talk about it. Honestly, let's bring it up. Let's talk about what's happening in the past. Let's talk about what's going on now. Let's talk about how we can normalize it. Let's talk about the double standards that exist. I remember this is one of those vivid memories when we had our third child. We have twins, and then we have a seven-year age gap. We've got twins, and then we have a young one. She's in kindergarten right now. Twins are middle school. She's in kindergarten. And this is, she's probably five months old. We're going for the first day of the legislative session in January in Kentucky. I'm the Senate minority leader at the time. We have a child care, just like Snafu, everything's going wrong. My wife can't miss one of these meetings at work and that's where I think I said, Hey, look, it's the first day of the session. But we can't even vote on Bill's the first day in the session in Kentucky because they haven't been introduced yet. So I said, I'm just going to take her with me. It'll be great. Like, it'll be fine. Don't worry about it. I'll take her with me. We'll be fine. So I strap on the baby Bjorn and take her to work, right? And the amount of like praise and the amount of ooze and oze, it was both nice. And it was like, wait a second, this shouldn't happen. I'm not doing anything special. And by the way, if a woman had been doing this, you'd probably be like, get your act together. Instead, I'm doing it. You're going, Oh, look, isn't he a great dad? It's like, no, no, I'm just a dad. who helps because I have a wife whose career is important too. And sometimes we're gonna have things where I've got something important to do is she might have to bring a kid to work or work from home or do that sort of thing. And that should be okay. Right, we shouldn't put all this on women like if you don't have this completely buttoned up 100% of the time somehow you're not doing your job. And so I think we have to talk about it in these real ways and these real ways that parents experience because one kid's gonna have the stomach flu or one kid's gonna, you know, swallow a crown or something, you know, like the something crazy is gonna happen. Both parents have to pitch in and help. Let's normalize it. It, you know, it shouldn't be a big deal. to have women in leadership roles, in responsible roles. It should be a big deal not to have women in big roles and in leadership roles. And then we should talk about just all the natural things we have to talk about. Let's elevate women and let's recognize that when you have working parents, you know, you've got to do things and talk about it.",
