    "turn_text": "I'm up. I'm getting up. I'm getting up where I'm coming. But he was from Appalachia. So he was from the eastern part of our state, the southeastern part of Kentucky was born in Hazard, Kentucky. Grew up the first part of his life in Alsley County, which was still one of the poorest counties. It was a really smart guy, but not a lot of opportunity back then. And that part of Kentucky for people, managed to kind of get a little bit of schooling to that sort of thing. But then World War II came along. He fought in World War II, like so many veteran stories, then came back, went to the University of Kentucky, went to law school at the University of Kentucky, but had two kids that time, not my mom, but had two kids, one of them got sick. And at that time in the late 40s, what they said was, you know, you've got to go somewhere high and dry. So he took the entire family out to the mountains in Arizona. Um, and the only job available for him in the mountains, Arizona was as a school teacher for every grade of school in a schoolhouse in Greer, Arizona. That's what he did for a year. Uh, his son, my uncle got better. They came back and he didn't know what to do. He had a law degree, you know, the time when not many people did, he had to say, so he started a law firm. And he started his own business and in a firm that still exists today. And so, you know, saw that my dad is also a lawyer. My mom was a teacher, although she didn't work most of the time. I was growing up. What I think you asked about creative households. My mom was an art teacher. So if you think of creativity, that's my mom. My dad is the complete opposite side of the brain. very logical, very data-driven. I would not call him a high EQ guy while being a pretty high IQ guy. He's so low on his emotional intelligence we can openly make fun of him for it and he's okay with it because his emotional intelligence is that low. But he and my mom kind of make a package, but also when you said creativity, runners with people like my dad or people like my brother-in-law is a wonderful guy and an engineer. Creativity doesn't have to mean you're good at music or you're good at art or you're good at expressive writing. You know, my dad, I think, is an incredibly creative guy when it comes to solving certain types of problems. And so I think you see creativity in lots of different ways. And when you talk about entrepreneurship, that can manifest itself. Most of our entrepreneurs aren't opening art galleries. Um, they're opening up businesses that deal with practical needs that people want addressed and how they creatively start that business, get it going, keep it going. Um, they're solving all sorts of problems that you might not again, you might not be thinking of an artist. I think we have to expand our definition of",
