    "turn_idx": 53,
    "turn_text": "When they redrew the districts, they drew them in a different way. And so it enabled me to stay. And what I found along the way is the Kentucky legislature is a part-time legislature. So I still practiced law while I was in the legislature. I was a practicing attorney, although I wouldn't call it a full-time practicing attorney, but at least a part-time practicing lawyer for the entire 10 years I was in the state Senate, and then ran for Congress at the end of it. But it was a great way to get into it and to discover what policy meant. I found out how much I love being a legislator along the way. My wife was able to follow her career a little bit. She's got an entrepreneurial spirit. She's working for young brands. basically was given the opportunity to move to Texas for a job there, but decided that's not what she wanted to do is best for our family. So she kind of started her own consulting business here. Then went from that and went back into a company that she was consulting for and has had some good career moves. It's been good for us. It's been a good journey. But I think that's how I got into it was just that kind of story saying, I'm going to do something to help getting into it, not thinking you're going to do it long. I really love it. I love the work of helping people. All the things I love about practicing law, like they're all there and being a legislator. You deal with laws and deal with statutes, you argue, you testify, you almost depose people when they're in front of your committees, and you get to help people, which is a really cool thing.",
