  {
    "episode_id": 74994,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74994.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 54,
    "turn_text": "Well, I love it. That leads me to my next question, actually. So I read that you remember. We just talked about this, the historically Black Colleges and Universities Caucus. And for those of you that don't know, that are listening, the Congressional Bipartisan HBCU Caucus was founded by Congresswoman Alma Adams on April 28, 2015. The caucus works to promote and protect the interests of HBCUs by creating a national dialogue, educating members of Congress and their staffs about the impacts, the issues impacting HBCUs, drafting meaningful bipartisan legislation to address the needs of HBCUs. So, you wrote a law that allows Simmons College, Louisville's only HBCU to lower tuition costs for its students, and we started as teacher training program, putting more black teachers into classrooms across Louisville and Kentucky. So, I'm just really curious of all the things we could work on in the universe. There's so many problems here, and I'm sure people at the people in Kentucky have a lot going on, but What happened around you that you saw this gap and wanted to fix something like that? Because I just am looking at you like, OK, what?",
