  {
    "episode_id": 74994,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/4/74994.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 55,
    "turn_text": "I get it. And I take that as a compliment. I think it's several things. First of all, I mean, I believe in the importance of HBCUs. I believe in the importance of black led educational institutions. I think it's also important to recognize not just the history of HBCUs but our own history and education. I mentioned my grandfather who was not just one of my best friends but really one of my heroes and how the GI Bill helped him. Let's talk about it was basically like winning the lottery if you were a black soldier. and got GI Bill benefits after World War II. And then look what happened. Neither the University of Kentucky nor the University of Louisville were integrated at the end of World War II. There wasn't a place for soldiers to go who fought for their country. I'm not telling you anything you don't know. And so I think recognizing that history, recognizing the importance of HBCUs. We're lucky to have two in Kentucky, one in Louisville. Because as you know, in HBCU, you can't go out and create one. So if you're a city right now and you don't have an HBCU, too bad. You had to be in existence before, I think it's what, 1964, to be an HBCU. And with Simmons College here, we have it. And Simmons College had gone through a period where it was basically shut down. And we had an amazing group of people here led by Dr. Kevin Cosby, who said we can revitalize Louisville's HBCU. They've gotten it done. Think about what it does for our city. If we can have a thriving HBCU in the middle of Louisville, it is a win-win-win for every person and every part of our community. But through one of those quirks in the law, they were prevented still from having a program where black teachers could graduate from Simmons College. Now, there are rings of data out there. We can talk about personal experience and anecdotes all day long, but let's also talk about the data of how important it is to have black teachers in the classroom, how this benefits students. And the fact that one of our two HBCUs could not offer a teacher training program, I thought was basically criminal, negligent at best, maybe worse than that. But so we wanted to fix it, right? Making sure that HBCUs get the funding that I believe they deserve, recognizing the role that systemic racism has played in education in this country. And how HBCUs can be a healing outlet for that, I saw that You know, I've learned about it. I continue to educate myself about it. And of course, did something about it on the state level and hope to add my voice and my efforts on the federal level as well.",
